+++
title: Tigris
description: River in Turkey, Iraq, and Syria
hook: The Tigris is named for being in a hurry. Sumerian scribes called it
  "the swift river" to set it against the Euphrates next door, whose leisurely
  pace left it dropping silt until it had built its bed up higher than its
  neighbour's.
standfirst: The eastern of the two great rivers that define Mesopotamia, the
  Tigris runs 1,900 km (1,200 mi) from Lake Hazar in Turkey to the Persian
  Gulf, past Mosul, Tikrit, Samarra and Baghdad. It has been named in a dozen
  languages, worshipped as a god, dammed, and used as a weapon.
key-facts:
  - name: How long
    value: 1,900 km (1,200 mi)
  - name: Where it starts
    value: Lake Hazar at Gölardı, Turkey, 1,150 m (3,770 ft) up in the Armenian
      Highlands
  - name: Where it ends
    value: The Shatt al-Arab, and on into the Persian Gulf
  - name: Countries it crosses
    value: Turkey, Syria, Iraq
  - name: Land it drains
    value: 375,000 km2 (145,000 sq mi)
  - name: How much water it carries
    value: 1,014 m3/s (35,800 cu ft/s) on average, between a low of 337 m3/s
      (11,900 cu ft/s) and a high of 2,779 m3/s (98,100 cu ft/s)
pull-quotes:
  - text: when I was by that great river the Tigris
    attribution: Daniel, in the Book of Daniel
    section: religion-and-mythology
timeline:
  - when: around 2900 B.C.
    what: A canal dug from the Tigris irrigates the city of Lagash.
  - when: around 1858
    what: Baháʼu'lláh writes The Hidden Words while walking the banks of the
      Tigris during his exile in Baghdad.
  - when: 1932 to 1959
    what: The river features on the coat of arms of Iraq.
  - when: "2014"
    what: Representatives of Iraq and Turkey agree the "Geneva Consensus On Tigris
      River", a Plan of Action on exchanging and calibrating data about the
      river's flows, at a meeting in Geneva organized by the Strategic
      Foresight Group.
  - when: February 2016
    what: The United States Embassy in Iraq and Prime Minister Haider al-Abadi
      warn that Mosul Dam could collapse, putting between 500,000 and 1.5
      million people at risk of drowning.
