The !!Tigris!! (see [[ below | #etymology ]]) is the eastern of the two great [[ rivers | river ]] that define [[ Mesopotamia ]]; the [[ Euphrates ]] is the other. It comes down from the mountains of the [[ Armenian Highlands ]], flows south through the [[ Syrian | syrian-desert ]] and [[ Arabian Deserts | arabian-desert ]], and merges with the [[ Euphrates ]] before reaching the [[ Persian Gulf ]].

On the way it passes historical cities: [[ Mosul ]], [[ Tikrit ]], [[ Samarra ]] and [[ Baghdad ]]. Archaeological sites stand along it, and so do ancient religious communities, among them the [[ Mandaeans ]], who use the river for [[ baptism | masbuta ]]. In ancient times the Tigris nurtured the [[ Assyrian Empire | assyria ]], with remnants such as the relief of [[ King Tiglath-Pileser | tiglath-pileser-i ]].

Today the river faces geopolitical instability, dam projects, poor water management and climate change, and these raise concerns about its sustainability. Efforts to protect and preserve the river's legacy are ongoing, with local archaeologists and activists working to safeguard its future.

== Etymology

The name is among the oldest still in daily use, and it has been worn shorter by every language that borrowed it. The chain runs from Sumerian 𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼 (Idigna) into Akkadian 𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼 (Idiglat), from there into Old Persian 𐎫𐎡𐎥𐎼𐎠 (Tigrā), then into Ancient Greek Τίγρης (Tígrēs) and its alternative form Τίγρις (Tígris), and finally into Latin as Tigris.

The [[ Ancient Greek ]] form //Tigris// (Τίγρις) probably derived from //*id (i)gina//, "running water".[[ ^5 ]] The Sumerian term can be interpreted as "the swift river", a name that contrasts the Tigris with its neighbour the Euphrates, whose leisurely pace caused it to deposit more [[ silt ]] and build up a higher bed than the Tigris. The Sumerian form was borrowed into [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] as //Idiqlat//, and from there into the other [[ Semitic languages ]]: compare Hebrew: חִדֶּקֶל‎, [[ romanized | romanization-of-hebrew ]]: //Ḥîddéqel//; Jewish Babylonian Aramaic: דיגלת‎, דיקגלת‎, romanized: //diqlāṯ// or //diglāṯ//; Classical Syriac: ܕܩܠܬ‎, romanized: //Deqlāṯ//; Arabic: دِجلَة, [[ romanized | romanization-of-arabic ]]: //Dijlah//.[[ ^6 ]]

In [[ Kurdish languages ]] it is known as //Ava Mezin//, "the Great Water".[[ ^7 ]]

The name of the Tigris in languages that have been important in the region:

!!Language!! / !!Name for Tigris!!

[[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]] / 𒁇𒄘𒃼, //Idiqlat//
[[ Arabic ]] / دِجلَة, //Dijlah//; حُدَاقِل, Ḥudāqil
[[ Aramaic ]] / דיגלת, //Diglath//
[[ Armenian | armenian-language ]] / Տիգրիս, //Tigris//, Դգլաթ, //Dglatʿ//
[[ Greek | ancient-greek ]] / [[ ἡ Τίγρης | https://incubator.wikimedia.org/wiki/Wp/grc/Τίγρης ]], -ητος, //hē Tígrēs, -ētos//; ἡ, ὁ Τίγρις, -ιδος, //hē, ho Tígris, -idos//
[[ Hebrew | hebrew-language ]] / חִדֶּקֶל, //Ḥiddéqel//[[ ^8 ]]
[[ Hurrian | hurrian-language ]] / //Aranzah//[[ ^9 ]]
[[ Persian | persian-language ]] / Old Persian: 𐎫𐎡𐎥𐎼𐎠 //Tigrā//; [[ Middle Persian ]]: //Tigr//; Persian: دجله //Dejle//
[[ Sumerian | sumerian-language ]] / 𒁇𒄘𒃼 //Idigna/Idigina//
[[ Syriac | syriac-language ]] / ܕܸܩܠܵܬܼ //Deqlaṯ//
[[ Turkish | turkish-language ]] / //Dicle//
[[ Kurdish | kurdish-languages ]] / Dîcle, Dijlê, دیجلە

== Geography

The Tigris is 1,750 km (1,090 mi) long. It rises in the [[ Taurus Mountains ]] of eastern [[ Turkey ]], about 25 km (16 mi) southeast of the city of [[ Elazığ ]] and about 30 km (19 mi) south of the valley of the Euphrates, so the two rivers that define Mesopotamia begin within a morning's walk of each other. The Tigris then runs 400 km (250 mi) through Southeastern Turkey before forming part of the [[ Syria-Turkey border ]]. That stretch of 44 km (27 mi) is the only part of the river located in Syria.[[ ^3 ]] Some of its affluences are Garzan, Anbarçayi, [[ Batman | batman-river ]], and the [[ Great | great-zab ]] and the [[ Little Zab ]].[[ ^10 ]]

Close to its confluence with the Euphrates the river stops being one river. The artificial [[ Shatt al-Hayy ]] branches off first, to join the Euphrates near [[ Nasiriyah ]]; then the Shatt al-Muminah and [[ Majar al-Kabir ]] branch off to feed the [[ Central Marshes ]]. Further downstream two other [[ distributary channels | distributary-channel ]] leave the river, the Al-Musharrah and [[ Al-Kahla ]], to feed the [[ Hawizeh Marshes ]]. The main channel continues southwards and is joined by the Al-Kassarah, which drains the Hawizeh Marshes again. Finally the Tigris joins the Euphrates near [[ al-Qurnah ]] to form the [[ Shatt-al-Arab ]]. It has not always ended this way: according to [[ Pliny | pliny-the-elder ]] and other ancient historians, the Euphrates originally had its outlet into the sea separate from that of the Tigris.[[ ^11 ]]

[[ Baghdad ]], the capital of [[ Iraq ]], stands on the banks of the Tigris, and the port city of [[ Basra ]] straddles the Shatt al-Arab. In ancient times many of the great cities of [[ Mesopotamia ]] stood on or near the river, drawing water from it to irrigate the civilization of the [[ Sumerians | sumer ]]. [[ Nineveh ]], [[ Ctesiphon ]] and [[ Seleucia | seleucia-on-the-tigris ]] were Tigris-side cities, and the city of [[ Lagash ]] was irrigated by the Tigris through a canal dug around 2900 B.C.

== Navigation

In a largely desert country the river is a road, and it has long been an important transport route. Shallow-draft vessels can go as far up as Baghdad, but for transport downstream from [[ Mosul ]] rafts have historically been needed.[[ ^12 ]][[ ^13 ]][[ ^14 ]]

== Management and water quality

The Tigris is heavily dammed in Iraq and Turkey, both to water the arid and semi-desert regions along the river valley and to hold back floods. Iraq needed the second as much as the first: the river has historically been notoriously prone to flooding after the April melting of snow in the Turkish mountains. [[ Mosul Dam ]] is the largest dam in Iraq.

Recent Turkish damming of the river has been the subject of some controversy, for both its environmental effects within Turkey and its potential to reduce the flow of water downstream. Water from both rivers is used as a means of pressure during conflicts.[[ ^15 ]]

In 2014 a major breakthrough was achieved in developing consensus between multiple stakeholder representatives of Iraq and Turkey on a Plan of Action for promoting exchange and calibration of data and standards pertaining to Tigris river flows. The consensus, known as the "Geneva Consensus On Tigris River", was reached at a meeting organized in [[ Geneva ]] by the think tank [[ Strategic Foresight Group ]].[[ ^16 ]]

Two years later the talk was of collapse rather than calibration. In February 2016 the [[ United States Embassy in Iraq ]] and the [[ Prime Minister of Iraq ]] [[ Haider al-Abadi ]] both issued warnings that [[ Mosul Dam ]] could fail.[[ ^17 ]] The United States warned people to evacuate the floodplain of the Tigris, because between 500,000 and 1.5 million people were at risk of drowning by [[ flash flood ]] if the dam collapses, and because [[ Mosul ]], [[ Tikrit ]], [[ Samarra ]] and [[ Baghdad ]] were all at risk.[[ ^18 ]]

== Religion and mythology

In [[ Sumerian mythology | sumerian-religion ]] the Tigris was created by the god [[ Enki ]], who filled the river with flowing water.[[ ^19 ]]

In [[ Hittite | hittites ]] and [[ Hurrian ]] mythology the river is not made by a god but is one. //[[ Aranzah ]]// (or //Aranzahas// in the [[ Hittite | hittite-language ]] nominative form) is the Hurrian name of the Tigris River, which was deified. He was the son of [[ Kumarbi ]] and the brother of [[ Teshub ]] and [[ Tašmišu ]], one of the three gods spat out of Kumarbi's mouth onto Mount Kanzuras. Later he colluded with [[ Anu ]] and the [[ Teshub ]] to destroy Kumarbi ([[ The Kumarbi Cycle | song-of-kumarbi ]]).

The Tigris appears twice in the [[ Old Testament ]]. In the [[ Book of Genesis ]] it is the third of the [[ four rivers | rivers-of-paradise ]] branching off the river issuing out of the [[ Garden of Eden ]].[[ ^8 ]] In the [[ Book of Daniel ]], [[ Daniel | daniel-biblical-figure ]] states that he received one of his visions "when I was by that great river the Tigris".[[ ^20 ]]

The river is also mentioned in Islam in Sunan Abi Daud 4306.[[ ^21 ]] The tomb of [[ Imam Ahmad Bin Hanbal ]] and [[ Syed Abdul Razzaq Jilani ]] is in Baghdad, and the flow of the Tigris restricts the number of visitors. [[ Baháʼu'lláh ]], the founder of the [[ Baháʼí Faith ]], wrote //[[ The Hidden Words ]]// around 1858 while he walked along the banks of the Tigris during his exile in Baghdad.

The Tigris featured on the [[ coat of arms of Iraq ]] from 1932 to 1959.

